latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Initialization:\n   a. Create a deep copy of the input grid.\n   b. Calculate the total number of black squares.\n   c. Calculate the target count for each color (total black squares / 4, rounded down).\n   d. Initialize a color counter dictionary for colors 1-4.\n\n2. Identify Special Squares:\n   a. Create lists for isolated squares, edge squares, and corner squares.\n   b. Identify vertical lines (consecutive black squares in a column).\n\n3. Initial Color Assignment:\n   a. Assign colors to isolated squares first, prioritizing color balance.\n   b. For vertical lines:\n      - If length > 2, use alternating colors (e.g., 1-2-1-2 or 3-4-3-4).\n      - If length <= 2, assign the same color if it doesn't create conflicts.\n   c. Assign colors to corner squares, ensuring they differ from adjacent squares.\n   d. Fill in remaining edge squares, prioritizing under-represented colors.\n\n4. Color Presence Check:\n   a. Identify any missing colors (1-4).\n   b. For each missing color, find a suitable square to change its color:\n      - Prioritize squares that don't create new conflicts.\n      - Prefer changing over-represented colors.\n\n5. Balance Adjustment:\n   a. Calculate the difference between the most and least used colors.\n   b. While the difference > 1:\n      - Identify the most and least used colors.\n      - Find a square with the most used color that can be changed to the least used color without creating conflicts.\n      - Update the color counters.\n\n6. Conflict Resolution:\n   a. Scan the grid for adjacent same-color squares (horizontally and vertically).\n   b. For each conflict:\n      - Determine which square to change (prefer changing the one with more same-color neighbors).\n      - Change it to the least used color that doesn't create new conflicts.\n      - Update the color counter.\n\n7. Vertical Alignment Enhancement:\n   a. Scan each column from top to bottom.\n   b. Where possible, without creating conflicts or severe imbalance, adjust colors to improve vertical alignment.\n\n8. Final Check and Adjustment:\n   a. Ensure all rules are satisfied (no conflicts, all colors present, balanced as much as possible).\n   b. If any rule is violated, identify the cause and make minimal adjustments to satisfy all conditions.\n\n9. Return the transformed grid.\n\nThis plan addresses all the key aspects of the challenge:\n- It prioritizes vertical alignment while allowing flexibility to ensure all rules are met.\n- It handles special cases (isolated, edge, and corner squares) explicitly.\n- It ensures all colors are present by checking and adjusting early in the process.\n- It aims for color balance through initial assignment and subsequent adjustments.\n- It resolves conflicts systematically.\n- The final check and adjustment step ensures that all rules are satisfied in the end.\n\nThis approach should be general enough to handle various input configurations while adhering to all the specified rules of the challenge."
